<http://data.silknow.org/event/201eb9dd-35b1-3d5f-9740-b0926ef9e760>
        a                              ecrm:E8_Acquisition ;
        rdfs:comment                   "The Chelsea Carpet is so called because the Museum purchased it from a dealer in Chelsea (in southwest London), named Alfred Cohen, in 1890. Aside from this, the carpet's provenance is not known, but its quality of design and production strongly suggests a court-level workshop in early sixteenth-century Iran."@en ;
        ecrm:P22_transferred_title_to  <http://data.silknow.org/actor/f0577f91-f887-3019-bf88-f9e5ba019390> ;
        ecrm:P24_transferred_title_of  <http://data.silknow.org/object/0d2e1583-6f15-3a15-b689-53d24331bfb1> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "The Chelsea Carpet is so called because the Museum purchased it from a dealer in Chelsea (in southwest London), named Alfred Cohen, in 1890. Aside from this, the carpet's provenance is not known, but its quality of design and production strongly suggests a court-level workshop in early sixteenth-century Iran."@en .

